          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            from (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+space"}> pointed
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            which assign
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatized
            by a small set of natural conditions, called the
            %em Eilenberg-Steenrod axioms
            (
            %a{:href => "#EilenbergSteenrod52"}> Eilenberg-Steenrod 52, I.3
            ), see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#TheEilenbergSteenrodAxioms"} below
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#EilenbergSteenrod52"} Eilenberg-Steenrod 52, I.3 Axiom 7
            %em generalized (co)homology theories
            (
            %a{:href => "#Whitehead62"}> Whitehead 62
            ) or
            = succeed "." do
              %em extraordinary (co)homology theories
          %p
            Examples include
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            (
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/topological+K-theory#AtiyahHirzebruch61"}> Atiyah-Hirzebruch 61, 1.8
            ),
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+cohomology+theory"} cobordism cohomology theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+homology"} generalized homology
          %p
            Notice that, while the terminology “generalized cohomology” is standard in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            with an eye towards
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            %em other
            and
            %em further
            generalizations of the axioms that all still deserve to be and are called “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"}> cohomology
            ”. For instance dropping the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension+isomorphism"} suspension
            axiom leads to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            and dropping the “homotopy axiom” (and taking the domain spaces to be
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            %em just
            discarding the dimension axiom. For lack of a better term, we say “generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology” here.
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Whitehead62"}> Whitehead 62
            ) it was observed that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            induces a generalized homology theory. The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} Brown representability theorem
            (
            %a{:href => "#Brown62"}> Brown 62
            ) asserts that every generalized (co)homology arises this way, being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} represented
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+spectra"} mapping spectra
            into/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
            with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phantom+maps"} phantom maps
          %p
            On the other hand, if one refines the concept of a generalized homology theory from taking values in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+object"} graded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            to taking values in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            then it does become equivalent to the concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %em spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "excisive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor#SpectrumObjects"} excisive functor – Examples – Spectrum objects
          %p
            This means that from a perspective of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+spectra"} (∞,1)-category of spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted
            generalized Eilenberg-Steenrod cohomology is the intrinsic cohomology of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} tangent (∞,1)-topos
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parameterized+spectra"} parameterized spectra
          .standout
            %p
              Generalized Eilenberg-Steenrod cohomology is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(X)= H(X,E)
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+object"} spectrum object
          %h2#TheEilenbergSteenrodAxioms Definition
          %p
            This sections states the classical formulation of the Eilenberg-Steenrod axioms due to (
            %a{:href => "#EilenbergSteenrod52"}> Eilenberg-Steenrod 52, I.3
            ) in terms of concepts from classical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-pairs"} CW-pairs
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cones"} mapping cones
          %p
            More abstractly, via the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical model structure on topological spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushouts"} homotopy pushouts
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category+theory"} (infinity,1)-category theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#AbstractFormulation"} below
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p There are two versions of the statement of the axioms:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a{:href => "#ReducedCohomology"} Reduced cohomology
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a{:href => "#UnreducedCohomology"} Unreduced cohomology
                \.
          %p
            There are functors taking any reduced cohomology theory to an unreduced one, and vice versa. When some fine detail in the axioms is suitably set up, then this establishes an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
            between reduced and unreduced generalized cohomology:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#RelationBetweenReducedAndUnreduced"} Relation between reduced and unreduced cohomology
          %p
            The fine detail in the axioms that makes this work is such as to ensure that a cohomology theory is a functor on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite
            of the (pointed/pairwise)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+homotopy+category"} classical homotopy category
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                On the one hand,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi Top
                        %mi Quillen
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Top_{Quillen})
                may be presented by topological spaces homeomorphic to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complexes"} CW-complexes
                and with
                = succeed "-classes" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
            %li
              %p
                On the other hand,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi Top
                        %mi Quillen
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Top_{Quillen})
                may be presented by all topological spaces with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"} weak homotopy equivalences
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} inverted
          %p
            Notice however that “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+homotopy+category"}> classical homotopy category
            ” is already ambiguous. Pre Quillen this was the category of
            %em all
            topological spaces with homotopy equivalence classes of maps between them, and often generalized cohomology functors are defined on this larger category and only restricted to CW-complexes or required to preserve weak homotopy equivalences when need be (e.g.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Switzer75"} Switzer 75, p.117
          %p
            Moreover, historically, these conditions have been decomposed in several numbers of ways. Notably (
            %a{:href => "#EilenbergSteenrod52"}> Eilenberg-Steenrod 52
            ) originally listed 7 axioms for unreduced cohomology, more than typically counted today, but their axioms 1 and 2 jointly just said that we have a functor on topological spaces, axiom 3 was the condition for the connecting homomorphism to be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Switzer75"} Switzer 75, p. 99,100
          %p
            Finally, following the historical development it is common to state the exactness properties of cohomology functors in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cone"} mapping cone
            constructions. These are models for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+cofibers"} homotopy cofibers
          %p
            For these reasons, in the following we stick to two points of views: where we discuss cohomology theories as functors on topological spaces we restrict attention to those homeomorphic to CW-complexes. In a second description we speak fully abstractly about functors on the homotopy category of a given model category of
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %h3#ReducedCohomology Reduced cohomology
          %p
            Throughout, write
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                    %mi CW
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}_{CW}
            for the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphic
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complexes"} CW-complexes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Top
                    %mi CW
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top^{\ast/}_{CW}
            for the corresponding category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
          %p
            Recall (
            %a{:href => "final+functor#FiberProductsInASliceCategory"}> here
            ) that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Top
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top^{\ast/}
            are computed as colimits in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            (
            %a{:href => "Top#UniversalConstructions"}> here
            ) after adjoining the base point and its inclusion maps to the given diagram.
          #WedgeSumAsCoproduct.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
              is the
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+sum"} wedge sum
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∨
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee_{i \in I} X_i
          %p Write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Top
                    %mi CW
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Top
                    %mi CW
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Sigma \coloneqq S^1 \wedge (-)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  Top^{\ast/}_{CW} \longrightarrow Top^{\ast/}_{CW}
          %p
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+suspension"} reduced suspension
            functor.
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ab
                    %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab^{\mathbb{Z}}
            for the category of integer-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+abelian+groups"}> graded abelian groups
            \.
          #ReducedGeneralizedCohomology.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                reduced
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              (“
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+in+cohomology"}> pullback in cohomology
              ”)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Top
                      %mi CW
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi Ab
                      %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\tilde E^\bullet
                    \\;\colon\;
                    (Top^{\ast/}_{CW})^{op} \longrightarrow Ab^{\mathbb{Z}}
            %p
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complexes"}> CW-complexes
              ) to
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+abelian+groups"} graded abelian groups
              (“
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"}> cohomology groups
              ”), in components
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi f
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\tilde E
                    \\;\colon\;
                    (X \stackrel{f}{\longrightarrow} Y)
                    \\mapsto
                    (\tilde E^\bullet(Y)
                    \\stackrel{f^\ast}{\longrightarrow}
                    \\tilde E^\bullet(X))
                    \\,,
            %p
              and equipped with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
              of degree +1, to be called the
              = succeed "," do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension+isomorphism"} suspension isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\sigma
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\tilde E^{\bullet +1}(\Sigma -)
                    \\overset{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}
                    \\tilde E^\bullet(-)
            %p#AxiomsReduced such that:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %strong
                    (
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+invariance"}> homotopy invariance
                    )
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1,f_2 \colon X \longrightarrow Y
                  are two morphisms of pointed topological spaces such that there is a (base point preserving)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ≃
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1 \simeq f_2
                  between them, then the induced
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
                  of abelian groups are
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equal
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi f
                          %mn 1
                          %mo *
                        %mo =
                        %msubsup
                          %mi f
                          %mn 2
                          %mo *
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        f_1^\ast = f_2^\ast
                        \\,.
              %li#ReducedExactnessAxiom
                %p
                  %strong (exactness)
                  For
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon A \hookrightarrow X
                  an inclusion of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi Cone
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j \colon X \longrightarrow Cone(i)
                  the induced
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cone"} mapping cone
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exact sequence
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+abelian+groups"} graded abelian groups
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mover
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mo •
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Cone
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mover
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi j
                              %mo *
                        %msup
                          %mover
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mo •
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mover
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi i
                              %mo *
                        %msup
                          %mover
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mo •
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\tilde E^\bullet(Cone(i))
                        \\overset{j^\ast}{\longrightarrow}
                        \\tilde E^\bullet(X)
                        \\overset{i^\ast}{\longrightarrow}
                        \\tilde E^\bullet(A)
                        \\,.
            %p
              We say
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde E^\bullet
              is
              %strong additive
              if in addition
            %ul
              %li#WedgeAxiom
                %p
                  %strong
                    (
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+axiom"}> wedge axiom
                    )
                  For
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{X_i\}_{i \in I}
                  any set of pointed CW-complexes, then the canonical comparison morphism
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mover
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mo •
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mo ∨
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi I
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⟶
                        %munder
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi I
                        %msup
                          %mover
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mo •
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\tilde E^\bullet(\vee_{i \in I} X_i)
                        \\longrightarrow
                        \\prod_{i \in I} \tilde E^\bullet(X_i)
                %p
                  is an
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+sum"} wedge sum
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WedgeSumAsCoproduct"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
                  of its values on the wedge summands, .
            %p
              We say
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde E^\bullet
              is
              %strong ordinary
              if its value on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-sphere"} 0-sphere
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^0
              is concentrated in degree 0:
            %ul
              %li
                %strong (Dimension)
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mover
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mrow
                            %mo •
                            %mo ≠
                            %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝕊
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≃
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde E^{\bullet\neq 0}(\mathbb{S}^0) \simeq 0
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              of reduced cohomology theories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo •
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\eta \;\colon\; \tilde E^\bullet \longrightarrow \tilde F^\bullet
            %p
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
              between the underlying functors which is compatible with the suspension isomorphisms in that all the following
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+square"} squares commute
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mover
                                %mi E
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                              %mo •
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi η
                                  %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mover
                                %mi F
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                              %mo •
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
